Standalone anchored tabs, inline tab rename, divergence-only anchor indicator,
and return-to-anchor on close.

- Adds an explicit per-tab "anchored" flag to TabUIHelper (persisted across
  sessions like the existing custom title / anchored URL) so a standalone
  (ungrouped, unpinned) tab can be anchored on its own. helium::IsAnchoredTab
  honours the flag; helium::IsAnchorEligibleForUiFeatures gates the per-tab UI
  behaviors (rename / indicator / close) to standalone-anchored and grouped
  tabs, excluding pinned tabs.
- The vertical unpinned container lays groups and standalone anchored tabs out
  in a single ordered region above the separator (natural/model order), so
  standalone anchors interleave freely with groups. Dragging a tab across the
  separator anchors/un-anchors it, with the rest of the strip reflowing live as
  the dragged tab crosses the separator; the decision is applied only on a
  completed drop (TabDragTarget's end-of-drag carries a `completed` flag).
- Double-clicking an anchor-eligible tab opens an inline rename text field in
  the tab row (Enter / Esc / blur; empty resets to the page title). The custom
  name persists across restarts via the session extra-data path.
- The "/" anchor indicator is shown only once a tab has navigated away from its
  original anchored URL (a "return to anchor" hint) and refreshes immediately on
  navigation. The anchor URL is also captured for grouped tabs, skipping
  blank/NTP URLs so an empty new tab is never treated as anchored.
- The close button (and middle-click) on an anchor-eligible tab that has
  navigated away from its anchor first returns it to the anchor URL instead of
  closing; closing again at the anchor closes normally. Blank/NTP anchors never
  hijack the close.

